Chain consulted: Uni → AS1 → AS2 → ACM operators.

Findings:

1. Origin-side, server-generated 503. The acm-dl/2.3 front-end at 198.82.0.1 is intentionally shedding load due to capacity exhaustion at the origin. L3/L4 are healthy (ICMP, TCP/80, TLS/1.3 all succeed); the 503 is produced by the application itself.

2. Isolated to the acm-dl service. Other infrastructure in ACM's AS is healthy — this is not an AS-wide outage.

3. NOT a path/proxy injection. Independently corroborated:
   - My gateway (src 128.173.0.1) reproduced the identical 503 (Server: acm-dl/2.3, Content-Length: 24).
   - AS1 reproduced the identical 503 from its own loopback (4.2.2.1, ~74ms RTT) via a different source network.
   - Same body and Server header from three distinct vantage points rules out anything path-specific.
   - My gateway has no transparent proxy, no HTTP interception, no filtering rules — only outbound NAT. AS1 confirms no proxy/interception/filter on their side. ACM confirms no proxy/WAF/scrubber/rate-limiter in front of the service.

Your interpretation was correct: not a client-side or network reachability problem. The origin is reachable and actively returning 503 at the application layer.

Status: CANNOT (pending admin action on ACM's side). ACM operators have been notified; no ETA provided. Recommended action: retry later — the service is degraded, not down.

